From 80435730319b4b61bc96cd7aad357708b316f172 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Usty <usty@uase.tech>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 09:35:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix: iOS black screen - no App Transport Security config
 at all for a remotely-loaded app

Real, concrete asymmetry found: Android has an explicit
network_security_config.xml declaring every domain this app connects
to (carstrims.com, onrender.com, cloudinary.com) - iOS's Info.plist
had no NSAppTransportSecurity entry whatsoever.

This app's entire UI loads remotely via capacitor.config.ts's
server.url (there is no meaningful local bundle) - every screen the
person sees depends on this WKWebView successfully reaching
carstrims.com, its backend on onrender.com, and images on
cloudinary.com. With zero ATS configuration, iOS falls back to its
strictest default policy for all three. If any of them doesn't
perfectly satisfy Apple's default requirements (certain TLS/cipher
specifics, forward secrecy expectations that not every server
enables), iOS silently blocks the request at the network layer - no
error surfaces to the WebView or the person, just an empty page that
never paints anything. That reads as a stuck black screen, and the
existing splash-hide fix (already in this repo, hides the splash once
a real frame paints, with a 12s safety-net fallback) can't help here
either way: if the page never loads, the safety timer just reveals
the same empty black WebView 12 seconds later instead of immediately.

This exactly matches the reported symptom pattern - web and Android
both work, only iPhone shows this - since ATS is an iOS-only
enforcement mechanism with no equivalent on the other two platforms.

Added an NSExceptionDomains block for the three domains this app
actually depends on, mirroring Android's explicit domain list rather
than disabling ATS broadly (NSAllowsArbitraryLoads) - that keeps
HTTPS required and TLS 1.2 minimum for all three, just relaxes the
forward-secrecy requirement specifically, since that's the most
common way a legitimate, properly-secured server still fails Apple's
strictest default without actually being insecure.

Important caveat, stated plainly rather than promised as certain:
this is the most concrete, verifiable gap found for an iOS-only
black-screen symptom on a WKWebView-loaded remote app, and directly
explains the reported pattern - but confirming it's the actual root
cause (versus a contributing factor alongside something in the CI
build pipeline) requires a fresh TestFlight/production build with
this fix included, tested on a real device.
---
 ios/App/App/Info.plist | 39 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 39 insertions(+)

diff --git a/ios/App/App/Info.plist b/ios/App/App/Info.plist
index 2bb4d37..6db6077 100644
--- a/ios/App/App/Info.plist
+++ b/ios/App/App/Info.plist
@@ -61,5 +61,44 @@
 	<string>CARSTRIMS uses your location to show nearby dealers and vehicles, and to fill in your state and city when setting up your dealership or listing a vehicle.</string>
 	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
 	<true/>
+	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
+	<dict>
+		<key>NSExceptionDomains</key>
+		<dict>
+			<key>carstrims.com</key>
+			<dict>
+				<key>NSIncludesSubdomains</key>
+				<true/>
+				<key>NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads</key>
+				<false/>
+				<key>NSExceptionMinimumTLSVersion</key>
+				<string>TLSv1.2</string>
+				<key>NSExceptionRequiresForwardSecrecy</key>
+				<false/>
+			</dict>
+			<key>onrender.com</key>
+			<dict>
+				<key>NSIncludesSubdomains</key>
+				<true/>
+				<key>NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads</key>
+				<false/>
+				<key>NSExceptionMinimumTLSVersion</key>
+				<string>TLSv1.2</string>
+				<key>NSExceptionRequiresForwardSecrecy</key>
+				<false/>
+			</dict>
+			<key>cloudinary.com</key>
+			<dict>
+				<key>NSIncludesSubdomains</key>
+				<true/>
+				<key>NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads</key>
+				<false/>
+				<key>NSExceptionMinimumTLSVersion</key>
+				<string>TLSv1.2</string>
+				<key>NSExceptionRequiresForwardSecrecy</key>
+				<false/>
+			</dict>
+		</dict>
+	</dict>
 </dict>
 </plist>
-- 
